WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.280
Many irregular fibres start at type I hair cells, each wrapped in a cup-shaped

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.620
ending called a calyx.

3
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.960
Could this synapse help make them fast?

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.960
A type I cell is flask-shaped, almost enclosed by its calyx.

5
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.580
A type II cell is cylindrical, with small boutons.

6
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.940
Calyx-only fibres, and many irregular fibres, come from each organ's central zone.

7
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.500
Type I cells occur in both zones.

8
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.740
That is a population pattern.

9
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:36.193
A tempting shortcut says type I cells release no vesicles.

10
00:00:37.293 --> 00:00:40.513
Wrong. Both types release glutamate from vesicles.

11
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:46.953
In sustained release, type I cells in mature mouse tissue released about ten times

12
00:00:46.953 --> 00:00:48.173
less than type II cells.

13
00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:50.483
Now a second route.

14
00:00:51.423 --> 00:00:56.883
Non-quantal transmission is a change in calyx voltage with no vesicle fusion and no

15
00:00:56.883 --> 00:00:57.883
gap junctions.

16
00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:03.967
At a chemical synapse, a vesicle must fuse, glutamate must cross, and receptors must

17
00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:06.287
open. Each step adds delay.

18
00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:08.537
Pause and predict.

19
00:01:09.697 --> 00:01:12.777
Remove otoferlin, or a calcium channel that release needs.

20
00:01:13.677 --> 00:01:15.777
Can a type I cell still make its calyx fire?

21
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:27.990
Yes. Without either protein, type I cells still made the calyx fire, in mouse tissue

22
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:28.990
in a dish.

23
00:01:29.483 --> 00:01:33.303
The test was whether spikes survive, not whether the signal is normal.

24
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:35.573
How could that work?

25
00:01:36.193 --> 00:01:39.153
The cleft between the cell and its calyx is very narrow.

26
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:44.863
When the hair cell depolarizes, more potassium current flows out into that thin

27
00:01:44.863 --> 00:01:47.823
space. Potassium slowly builds up there.

28
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:53.687
Raise potassium in the cleft, and potassium's reversal potential at the calyx moves

29
00:01:53.687 --> 00:01:54.687
up.

30
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:59.840
With the other conductances held fixed, this shift tends to depolarize the calyx.

31
00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:03.913
The current also makes the cleft itself positive, quickly.

32
00:02:05.053 --> 00:02:08.773
Less potassium leaves the calyx, or some enters, so it depolarizes.

33
00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:14.927
We met potassium first, but in a model, the cleft potential acts first and potassium

34
00:02:14.927 --> 00:02:17.387
later. That order is a prediction.

35
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:19.967
Keep the evidence apart.

36
00:02:20.927 --> 00:02:23.387
Mouse knockouts and young rat calyces are measurements.

37
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.480
So are turtle recordings of both cells together, which found a slow coupling and a

38
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.480
much faster one.

39
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.880
Two routes leave a type I cell.

40
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:35.880
One waits for vesicles.

41
00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:37.520
One does not.
